  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 350 AM EDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nEarly morning RAP analysis indicates the mid level ridge over the \nPlains has been flattened by the trough making its way over the \nDakotas, highlighted nicely in GOES WV imagery. Back in the CWA, \nupper level diffluence, a subtle shortwave, and low level WAA are \nsupporting showers and weak thunderstorms noted on the radar mosaic \nover north-central Lake Superior and Keweenaw County. The 7/31 0Z \nARW seems to be capturing this the best, which would suggest these \ncontinue to progress southeast while mostly residing over the waters \nas they follow the CAPE gradient and low level wind flow. That said, \nthere is a 20-40% chance for showers and weak storms in the counties \nnear Lake Superior early this morning. Otherwise temps stay around \n10F above normal in the 60s through sunrise.\n\nThe rest of today and tonight focuses on the Dakota trough which \ndeepens as it moves over MN, IA, and WI. At the same time, a cold \nfront presses in from the northwest. Although much of the energy \nwith this system remains south of the area, continued low level \nmoisture and diurnal heating will develop scattered thunderstorms \nthis afternoon. Temps in the 70s to mid 80s above Tds in the mid 60s \nto low 70s (warmest T/Td in the east) support modest instability \n(1000-2000 J/kg). HREF mean PWATs between 1.6 to 1.8 overhead during \nthe day (a few deterministic solutions such as the RAP go as high as \n2.0 inches over the east) in the afternoon are above the NAEFS 90th \nclimatological percentile. Given that storm motion vectors are slow \nand warm cloud depths are at least around 10 kft, rainfall amounts \nwill be something to watch. The low shear environment indicates that \nwell organized strong to severe storms are not expected. That said, \nminor flooding in poor drainage areas is a concern, especially if \nstorms train over an area. HREF neighborhood probabilities of at \nleast 1 inch of rainfall are at 30% across most of the UP save for \nthe Keweenaw. There is up to a 50% chance of at least 1 inch of rain \nover the central/eastern UP where convergence along a Lake Superior \nlake breeze meets up with the more favorable environment. Mean QPF \nstorm totals for the day range from 0.25 to 0.5 inches, but \nlocalized higher amounts up to 1 to 2 inches are possible in the \ncentral and eastern UP. WPC does capture this risk with the entire \nUP highlighted in a marginal risk of exceeding flash flood guidance \n(at least 5% chance).\n\nExpect light rain to persist through Saturday morning as the cold \nfront continues to sag south. Dry weather should then settle in \nthrough the early part of next week as high pressure and upper level \nridging build in across the region. A low pressure system \napproaching from the west will bring a return to the active pattern \nin the middle part of next week with a chance (30-50%) of showers \nand thunderstorms. The forcing and instability look rather week \nduring this period; therefore, high impact weather is not \nanticipated.\n\nTemperatures warm back into the 80s for Monday and Tuesday with \nhumidity increasing on Tuesday ahead of an approaching cold front. \nShowers and thunderstorms may accompany the front on Tuesday but the \nrisk for organized convection remains uncertain at this point in \ntime, with global models showing lower instability and shear.",
